SM(298,{"n":"Exodus 20.1-6","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Exodus 20.1-6.mp3","d":3245.5,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.36,"We'll go ahead and get started."],[24.24,"We get started, we're picking up at Exodus chapter 20."],[28.72,"One of the more famous uh chapters in the Old Testament in the Torah."],[39.44,"Especially around these parts, if you follow the local news."],[44.96,"So um we'll be picking up there."],[50.0,"Um I could do the review relatively quickly because uh basically where we left off is Israel has made it to Mount Sinai, the Mountain of God, God has descended upon the top of Mount Sinai, Moses has gone up Mount Sinai, and now God is talking to Moses and is going to issue his covenant with the newly forming right nation of Israel."],[81.8,"Um so yeah, that's it that's the pretty quick recap that probably covers it as well as we need to."],[89.88,"Um as you can see from the header, if you already turned to Exodus 20, what I was saying was this is the Ten Commandments, so relatively well known um section."],[102.12,"So unless anybody has any leftover comments or questions or available hotel deals in Southwest Pennsylvania that I need to know about, we'll go ahead and pick up in uh Exodus chapter 20, verse 1."],[120.88,"Now the Lord spoke all these words saying, I am the Lord your God who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage."],[130.8,"You shall have no other gods before me."],[135.6,"We'll pause right there."],[138.24,"So, these are known as the Ten Commandments."],[143.2,"Here's the thing."],[145.36,"There's only nine of them."],[148.56,"I know you're wondering how that's possible."],[149.84,"But it's true"],[156.44,"In the actual text, there are nine things, nine commandments that are given."],[163.4,"And because of that, because there's actually only nine in the text itself, but it's always been called the Ten Commandments."],[173.4,"Different groups of interpreters divide them differently to make it ten."],[181.64,"And by different groups, I mean there's a sort of standard Jewish way of dividing the Ten Commandments."],[190.2,"There is a Roman Catholic way of dividing the Ten Commandments, there is a Lutheran way of dividing the Ten Commandments."],[201.04,"There is a most other Protestant way Protestants' way of dividing the Ten Commandments."],[208.88,"And the Orthodox way of dividing the Ten Commandments is actually closest to the Lutheran way."],[216.9,"Of dividing them."],[220.5,"So yeah. So part of this has helped."],[226.66,"Part of this has helped."],[228.02,"And this helps explain the Jewish way of dividing them by the fact that while we all know these as sort of modern American folks as the Ten Commandments, uh they were for more of their history actually referred to as the ten words."],[249.74,"Right in Greek that's deca, ten, logi or log."],[255.47,"Words, hence decalogue sometimes is used to refer to the ten words."],[261.55,"Now, if you call the ten words, that gives you a little flexibility because then they don't all have to be commandments."],[266.92,"So you can say something that's not a commandment is one of the ten."],[270.84,"That's exactly how the Jewish numbering works."],[273.4,"So the Jewish numbering takes, I am the Lord your God, or literally, I am Yahweh your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage."],[282.52,"They say that's the first one."],[283.4,"And you're like, well, that's not a commandment"],[284.16,"Uh-huh. They're like, well, it's a word."],[289.66,"So. And that words comes from, notice, the Lord spoke spoke all these words."],[297.6,"Saying. Now, all the other numberings, except for that Jewish numbering, are all based on the idea that they're commandments."],[308.95,"So you have the here in the new King James, it's you shall or you shall not."],[316.83,"We may remember the King James from when I was a kid."],[319.63,"Thou shalt not."],[321.77,"X, Y, Z."],[323.05,"That makes it a commandment."],[324.89,"Arguably So is that the reason why commandments rather than words?"],[330.53,"Like what about it?"],[332.21,"I mean, is that known the reason why it's written more well known as commandments now as opposed to just sticking with the ten words?"],[341.49,"Yeah. Yeah, it's it's hard to tell."],[345.97,"That's kind of lost in prehistory."],[347.97,"Because there's another factor here, and that's that in pretty much every form of Christianity, the Ten Commandments loom kind of large."],[357.94,"So you go and pick up any Protestant confession from the Reformation, they've got a section going through the Ten Commandments."],[365.46,"You pick up one of our Orthodox prayer books and go to the Guide for Confession."],[368.82,"Well, it starts with the Ten Commandments"],[369.1,"There's a section going through the Ten Commandments."],[371.5,"You pick up Roman Catholic."],[374.9,"So, Christianity, the Ten Commandments is considered this sort of basic building block, sort of the basic moral principles."],[383.3,"And it just hasn't historically had that role in Judaism."],[388.43,"They're certainly there."],[391.12,"But because Judaism, I think, you know, is focused on all the commandments."],[399.7,"They don't differentiate as much."],[403.08,"So I think a big part of that is coming out of the early church."],[408.68,"What are the parts of the Torah?"],[410.44,"This is something we're going to keep talking about as we go through it."],[411.56,"Because we're going to run into a whole lot more commandments than these"],[415.82,"What are the things that apply to everybody, meaning including the Gentiles?"],[420.3,"And what are the things that are just specifically for the Jewish people?"],[424.78,"Obviously, for rabbinic Judaism, they're not concerned about the Gentiles."],[431.35,"So they're just, all of this applies to them."],[433.59,"They don't differentiate as much."],[435.75,"But these are pretty much all things that would apply to everybody."],[441.67,"Because they're against idolatry, they're against pagan worship, they're against things like murder and adultery and stealing."],[448.41,"These are things that are wrong, not just based on someone being Jewish."],[454.03,"And so I think that probably, I mean, we don't have like, there's not a paper trail for me to prove this."],[461.37,"But I think that's why with as Christianity emerged, out of Judaism early on, this grouping here of commandments sort of came to hold the special place."],[477.76,"Because the stuff you get into right afterward."],[481.78,"You could look at just looking at the subheadings."],[484.2,"Chapter 21, Buying a Slave."],[488.14,"That's sort of right off the bat, like for early Christians, was like, okay, this doesn't."],[493.98,"Yeah, owners of animal."],[495.58,"Yeah. Like, this is stuff that. doesn't"],[498.58,"And so Gentile Christians, you know, this is where God starts, and you have this unit."],[505.48,"And so I think that's why it became so prominent."],[510.12,"And so the emphasis with it being words doesn't hold uh doesn't hold as well."],[517.13,"And it was looked at as these are commandments that apply to us."],[522.51,"These are ones we need to be concerned about."],[525.17,"More than the ones that immediately follow."],[529.65,"That's my theory."],[530.69,"It's believable. It's plausible."],[532.45,"Could be true."],[534.37,"Yeah. Um. Yeah."],[538.61,"So then basically, the other ways of getting to ten, you have to take one of the nine and split it into two."],[549.37,"Okay, so the two ways that that is done is uh either, and I know we haven't done it yet, but um you either split uh you shall not make for yourself an idol or a likeness of anything in heaven or the earth or under the earth, you shall not bow down to them or serve them into two different commandments."],[572.41,"Don't make idols and don't worship idols."],[575.77,"Which seems like a weird split."],[581.9,"Or you go to just skipping ahead to verse 17 for the sake of talking about this, uh, you shall not covet your neighbor's wife nor his house, neither shall you covet his field."],[596.66,"That's how it's translated the New King James."],[598.74,"So you separate where it says, where you get the you shall not covet."],[604.44,"So they separate you shall not covet your neighbor's wife as one commandment, and then you shall not covet all your other neighbors' stuff as a different commandment."],[615.48,"And again, that doesn't make a heck of a lot of sense."],[621.52,"Because between the fact that all of these are about coveting and the fact that you already had a commandment against adultery."],[628.32,"y. In terms of your neighbor's wife, wh that would need to be a separate commandment"],[634.18,"And the other problem with that, this is actually where the Roman Catholics split it."],[638.98,"So I'm kind of picking on them now."],[641.3,"But such is life."],[643.46,"Deuteronomy 5, the Ten Commandments are going to be repeated."],[647.78,"That's um they're not they're not totally identical."],[653.16,"I mean, they're the same commandments, but the wording is not absolutely identical."],[658.52,"And one of the places where that wording is different is in that last commandment, uh, where there you're not supposed to covet uh his uh house."],[673.16,"And then it lists everything else"],[677.44,"So if you divide the commandments there, then that means Deuteronomy 5 and Exodus 20 have a different ninth commandment."],[687.28,"So what's the answer, you may ask me, Father Stephen?"],[691.68,"And I say to you, I'm cool with it just being nine."],[697.34,"I mean, we understand what's being communicated here."],[700.62,"Is the numbering that important?"],[705.52,"Or is understanding what God is telling us to do and not to do."],[710.76,"If youre gonna put' in the classroom actually?"],[713.13,"I know you need an even number."],[713.17,"I know. I know."],[716.37,"Idol making idol worship"],[720.09,"Were you dividing the idols?"],[724.65,"Uh yes, actually."],[726.73,"Usually. Not always."],[731.77,"But yeah. Yeah."],[732.97,"And sometimes it's hard to tell, to be honest."],[736.11,"So something people don't take into account is when you're reading actual ancient Greek text, including the church fathers."],[743.21,"The actual manuscripts don't look like you buy a book in Greek now."],[750.33,"Like you say, like, oh, this is the original Greek text."],[753.13,"Because when you buy the original Greek text now, I just did scare quotes for the recording."],[758.21,"Uh, it has spaces between the words, and capital and lowercase letters and accent marks and things like this, which the actual manuscripts don't have."],[775.34,"So if, for example, you have a prayer book and they're going through the Ten Commandments, and there's no spaces between the words, and no punctuation at the end of sentences, how are how can you be totally sure where they're dividing it?"],[798.78,"You can kind of use context clues."],[801.18,"It seems like maybe he's dividing these two."],[804.46,"Possibly But I think this is an issue that we shouldn't get hung up on."],[813.46,"I think we should just look at what's trying to be communicated and not worry about the number."],[819.38,"Um I will still refer to it as the Ten Commandments, just because if I called it the nine commandments, everyone would look at me weird and not know what I'm talking about."],[827.7,"Um but you know, or you can take the ten words approach."],[834.66,"And just instead of saying Ten Commandments, say the ten words."],[838.66,"It sound pedantic"],[841.1,"How are you written in Hebrew?"],[842.7,"Isn't there like isn't it?"],[846.06,"This is tax in my memory, but I can recall a teaching or reading where it says thou shalt not kill."],[854.22,"It's kill no."],[855.66,"Well, it's the other way."],[856.54,"It's no kill."],[858.94,"If you wouldn't leave literally, so yeah."],[861.42,"So the thou shalt not."],[864.03,"If that's what you go by."],[866.03,"Um, which yeah, we already talked about what there's issues with that."],[871.63,"But in Hebrew, the particle low is used to negate a sentence."],[879.87,"So you don't write you you, and this is future tense, sort of."],[886.83,"There aren't really verb tenses in Hebrew, but the idea is you take the sentence uh you kill, and you put low."],[895.6,"Or like no in front of it."],[897.52,"Like no, you kill, no, you steal."],[900.58,"If you want to be like woodenly literal."],[902.76,"Like it negates whatever the sentence is by putting low at the front of it."],[908.84,"Um then, but then there are also positive, like honor your father and your mother doesn't have a low in front of it."],[918.94,"There's no thou shalt not there, but that's clearly one of the commandments."],[926.04,"So yeah. That's um."],[930.52,"I don't remember if I mentioned this last time, so I'll I'll mention it now."],[936.12,"I know I talked a little bit, but I said I was gonna go into it more later, about covenants and the cutting of covenants, and I think I talked about the Hittite treaties we have that are kind of similar to this."],[951.92,"People are nodding at me, so I must have talked about it a little."],[954.8,"And I talk about the two copies."],[958.08,"Okay. So we have this the two tables of the law."],[964.73,"The two tablets."],[966.81,"And right, we needed even numbers so we could split them evenly."],[970.01,"Or more commonly, actually, with the Ten Commandments, you get the first four on one side, and the other six on the other side, of the other tablet."],[978.65,"Um, and then or if you're in Mel Brooks'ory Hist of the the World, you have the 15 Commandments, and he drops one and then he says Ten Commandments."],[987.81,"But there's all this stuff drawn out about, well, like the first four, these are about, you know, God, and the second six, these are about your fellow human beings, and all this stuff, and all this stuff."],[1002.37,"The reality is it's two copies of the same thing."],[1007.25,"Because with the treaties in the pagan context, one copy of the treaty would be given to the king who was receiving it."],[1018.47,"The king of the nation, because he had to read it and follow it."],[1022.63,"And then another copy would be put at the feet of the idol of that nation's god in the temple, which was sort of like calling on that god to bear witness, to the covenant."],[1037.04,"Uh and so since Yahweh is both the king of Israel and the God of Israel, both of them end up getting put in the Ark of the Covenant, which we'll read about here in a little while."],[1049.6,"But which was mentioned earlier in the text, even though they hadn't told us about making it yet."],[1064.32,"So both copies are put at God's feet."],[1066.4,"So the two tablets are two copies of this, not half and half."],[1070.56,"They're also probably much smaller than you're thinking"],[1078.28,"This is going to be another Father Stephen Ruin Sunday school."],[1081.08,"I don't think I've pointed this out yet anywhere, but uh this is gonna break your hearts because you've you've seen Charles Neston with the big stone tablets."],[1089.9,"The big. Like he could barely barely hold them."],[1093.53,"If they were actual stone, he wouldn't have been able to hold them at that size."],[1097.75,"Same styrofoam, painted gray."],[1102.47,"Actual tablets, the kind of tablets like with Kudai for writing and stuff, uh that these would be on would be about this big."],[1111.75,"For the sake of the recording, the way they made clay tablets for writing was uh they were made in the palm of your hand."],[1113.83,"they That's why're kind of rounded on the back."],[1123.39,"So a person would grab clay, they'd smooth it out in their hand, so it was what would fit in the palm of your hand."],[1129.41,"And then with the other hand with a stylus while it was wet, you would write make the marks, the little wedge marks in it, write everything you were going to write."],[1140.89,"And then that would get fired, like in a kiln."],[1144.89,"And once you do that, those clay tablets will pretty much last forever."],[1148.73,"That's why we have so many of them now, in some cases, like 5,000 years after they were made."],[1155.53,"And they're still so durable, like you can throw one on the ground and it'll put a dent in the floor before it breaks itself."],[1162.77,"Like it's a very sturdy and durable thing."],[1165.98,"So we're gonna be told that these are written by God with his finger."],[1170.4,"Meaning because I say they're with a stylus."],[1172.26,"He's gonna use his finger to write it."],[1174.82,"But we're still talking about tablets."],[1176.98,"Now you may you may be thinking, well, how big is God's hand?"],[1183.53,"And uh I'll be sneaky and counter with how big is Jesus' hand?"],[1191.53,"Because as we've already talked about, that's who Moses is talking to."],[1196.1,"So we're talking about tablets that are like human palm sized."],[1199.62,"So relatively small"],[1201.58,"Two of them."],[1206.28,"So, which also makes sense because we're gonna get to the measurements of the Ark of the Covenant."],[1211.64,"And if they were like Charlton Heston size, they wouldn't have fit inside anyway."],[1216.68,"Um, so yes, so that's it's it's two copies."],[1226.52,"And I think we at least briefly mentioned a little bit of the format."],[1229.88,"So the format of those covenants was it starts out by this is a covenant issued, it's a treaty issued between a suzerain, like a great king, to one of his vassal kings."],[1244.21,"And it starts out by the suzerain, the high king saying, Here's who I am."],[1251.49,"Here's what I've done for you."],[1253.43,"And in the case of the pagan ones we found, it's usually like I've given you peace and economic prosperity."],[1259.99,"I defeated your enemies, these people, etc."],[1263.37,"etc. etc. And then it goes on to say, okay, now here's your responsibilities."],[1269.7,"Outlines the vassal king's responsibilities of the high king, which in the pagan ones are usually like, you need to give me this much tribute of slaves every year, this much tribute and oxen, this much tribute, and you know, whatever the harvest is."],[1285.0,"This much wheat, this much."],[1288.37,"And then uh at the end, it would have uh here's the good things that will happen for you if you keep this covenant and this treaty, and then here's the bad things that will happen to you if you do not right, keep this treaty."],[1305.89,"So we can already see right here at the beginning, this is kind of following that format."],[1311.49,"We get to the book of Deuteronomy, we're gonna see the whole book of Deuteronomy follows that format, like the whole thing."],[1317.49,"But so we're starting out with he identif this is why he identifies himself."],[1321.49,"I am Yahweh, your God."],[1324.19,"So he names himself, he identifies himself, and then he says, Who brought you out of the land of Egypt out of the house of slavery?"],[1329.79,"Here's what I did for you."],[1332.87,"And then he's gonna go forward and outline, now here's what's expected of you."],[1338.92,"Um that's gonna be right the commandments we're gonna read now."],[1343.16,"So you said vassal king, so yeah."],[1346.84,"So the this is like understood that the entire each person of the tribe of Israel is treated as like a king, as a as a vassal, yes, not like a serf."],[1357.56,"That's the kingdom of priests."],[1359.17,"The royal priesthood idea."],[1361.73,"That makes them elevates the meaning."],[1363.65,"This wouldn't have been given in a different context."],[1367.01,"It was just a king to a king, not a king to all the people of that kingdom."],[1371.19,"Okay. That that that is an important point."],[1373.17,"This isn't a covenant between God and Moses"],[1379.35,"This is a covenant between God and his people given through Moses."],[1383.83,"That's why like the New Testament is to describe Moses as the mediator of the covenant."],[1389.51,"But it's not a covenant between God and Moses, just with here's what Moses has to do."],[1394.81,"It's between God and his people."],[1398.01,"Mediated through through Moses."],[1407.41,"And so then we read what is arguably the first commandment."],[1412.53,"You shall have no other gods before me."],[1414.85,"So literally what that means, if you want to be woodenly literal, it's you will not have any other gods in my face."],[1426.29,"The word for God's face is usually translated in English as God's presence."],[1440.93,"In the place where I am."],[1447.69,"It is interesting and important to point out just how common that phrase about the face of God is in the Old Testament."],[1462.25,"Every time it talks about God's presence, it's actually the face of God."],[1470.49,"So when Jonah gets up and flees, it says he flees from the face of God."],[1480.17,"When priests are going to serve for the tabernacle of the temple, they're coming before the face of God."],[1488.65,"The face of the God who nobody has seen."],[1491.77,"So there's this constant tension in the Hebrew idiom."],[1497.37,"It's going to become important in a few chapters because we're going to talk about God speaking to Moses face to face."],[1503.93,"And then nine verses after it says that, God's going to say to Moses, no one can. see my face and live"],[1508.93,"And you're going to go, hmm?"],[1512.53,"So there's this tension about the face of God."],[1515.9,"That gets kind of covered over."],[1521.39,"But so notice this isn't saying no other gods exist."],[1528.11,"This isn't saying there aren't any, you know, the things that the nations are worshiping don't exist."],[1533.23,"They're not demons, they're not whatever, they're imaginary."],[1536.61,"We've already talked about it before."],[1537.81,"We already saw that when God said he was gonna judge all the gods of Egypt."],[1540.67,"We talked a little about that."],[1542.59,"But he's saying, You don't bring any of them into my presence."],[1551.53,"You can't worship God alongside these other things."],[1559.3,"Alongside these other things."],[1562.82,"And this is where all kinds of applications come."],[1567.54,"So, like what does Christ say?"],[1570.98,"No man can serve two masters."],[1574.34,"He's not talking about polygamy there."],[1577.38,"He says, no man can serve two masters."],[1580.1,"You'll love one and hate the other."],[1581.7,"Or hate one and love the other."],[1583.22,"You can't serve God in mammon."],[1586.68,"That serve there is talking about this."],[1589.1,"Service, worship. This is important."],[1595.54,"Because we often have in our heads, when we think about this commandment."],[1601.01,"Well, I don't worship any, I don't worship Zeus."],[1604.85,"Like, I don't worship the Hindu gods."],[1606.77,"I don't worship right."],[1609.89,"But there's a there's a different temptation here."],[1612.91,"This is not a commandment about, like people who worship the true God are good and everybody else is bad."],[1620.24,"This is about whether our loyalties are divided."],[1625.28,"Whether our loyalty and faithfulness to God is divided with something else."],[1632.26,"With something else."],[1636.86,"And Christ, for example, takes this to an extreme in a few places in the New Testament that aren't possible popular verses to read."],[1647.42,"Like when he says, He who loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me."],[1652.06,"He who loves son and daughter more than me is not worthy of me."],[1657.26,"And we think that's pretty harsh."],[1659.42,"Then you read the same stuff in St."],[1661.02,"Luke's gospel, and he says, unless you hate your father and your mother."],[1665.74,"And you're like, what?"],[1669.31,"Like, it's even that extent."],[1671.01,"Like, you can't have divided loyalties."],[1675.83,"That's what this commandment is about."],[1677.59,"And this is something that's really hard for modern people."],[1683.71,"Because think about all the times we take our Christian faith, and shall we say, nuance it based on other factors?"],[1698.56,"Well, I'm a Christian, but my son or my daughter or my brother or my sister does XYZ?"],[1711.18,"So do we say, well, I love that person, but what they're doing is wrong?"],[1718.46,"Or do we say, well, no, I mean, that's not really wrong."],[1727.34,"When we do that, we're loving that other person."],[1732.48,"And we're putting that not only alongside, but kind of above."],[1738.18,"Our loyalty to what God has said and what we should do."],[1742.12,"Or I know there's all that stuff in the gospels about a camel's going through the eyes of needles and you know not serving, trying to serve God and mammoth, but you know, a guy's got to make a living."],[1752.52,"And uh, you know, we are in a capitalist society, and uh my investments are doing really well right now, and right, and so we sort of nuance."],[1762.38,"I mean, there's nothing wrong with that."],[1763.43,"I mean, it's like you know, when I get rich, God's blessing me."],[1766.05,"Like. There's a lot of nuancing, that happens."],[1772.57,"And it's always our Christian faith that gets kind of bent and warped rather than us bending how we think about things or bending what we do or sacrificing something else, we just sort of move our faith around."],[1789.13,"So we do it a little more subtly."],[1790.83,"It was very unsubtle in the old testament."],[1792.75,"They build an altar to Yahweh, and then they build an altar to Baal right next to it, and that's pretty unsubtle."],[1797.95,"But we we do the same thing just in in very more subtle, clever, nuanced ways."],[1806.17,"So that we can pretend we're not doing it."],[1808.89,"Because it's not as gross and in your face."],[1817.45,"So verse 4."],[1823.45,"This is why I didn't try to tackle this at the end of uh the last Bible study, by the way."],[1830.49,"You shall not make for yourself an idol or a likeness of anything in heaven above or in the earth beneath, or in the waters under the earth."],[1840.89,"Yeah, let's go ahead and pause there."],[1842.73,"So this may or they may not be separate from the next sentence."],[1848.15,"Now notice it's saying, don't make an idol."],[1849.87,"'t make an idol. Don"],[1857.35,"This uh some folks, some folks think they're taking this very literally, and they're not."],[1870.39,"They're not. So to take this very literally would actually be this is talking about idols."],[1875.51,"And so we, and we'll do this in a minute."],[1877.83,"We'll talk about how idolatry works and what that actually is."],[1881.61,"But there are some people who will say, well, no, this is this is talking about images."],[1888.57,"Like, say, icons in an Orthodox church."],[1891.98,"Or, you know, some of the folks I grew up around."],[1896.11,"You make a you make a movie with Jesus in it."],[1900.21,"That's breaking the second commandment."],[1902.59,"Um, even an animated one."],[1905.41,"We still had flannel graphs though in Sunday school."],[1908.21,"Oh, yeah. You know, which which yes, which were a visual, but anyway."],[1912.61,"Um. So they they kind of want to take that approach."],[1920.35,"Here's the problem with that approach."],[1923.25,"If you're gonna go down that road, you have to really go down that road."],[1925.97,"Because what this says then is you can't make an image of anything."],[1931.89,"No more family photos."],[1936.9,"No portraits. No toys."],[1940.26,"No toys. You can't even do landscapes."],[1942.9,"Trees and stuff."],[1944.1,"Those are creatures."],[1947.19,"No visual representations of any kind."],[1949.59,"And here's where it gets really problematic."],[1951.67,"Here's where it gets really problematic."],[1953.83,"What about mental images?"],[1959.27,"You come to church and I read you the gospel reading."],[1965.81,"You picture Jesus in your head."],[1968.45,"Oh, just made an idol."],[1975.01,"So that interpretation doesn't make sense."],[1977.41,"So what is the word being translated as likeness?"],[1982.09,"How is it different?"],[1983.05,"Well, it's talking about it, it's talking about an idol."],[1985.37,"That's why the word idol is there."],[1988.03,"And it's actually, I think they had or here."],[1991.07,"Did they put or?"],[1992.35,"Yeah, they put or."],[1994.19,"It's actually an idol in the likeness."],[1997.71,"Okay. An idol in the likeness."],[2000.85,"It's the or may seem like these are two separate things."],[2004.03,"You can't make an idol, and you also can't make, you know, a likeness."],[2007.71,"Yeah, that's what it's."],[2008.59,"You can't have a swan statue in your garden."],[2010.83,"Yeah. right Even if it's not an idol."],[2012.37,"Yeah. Which again, I don't think anyone really."],[2016.45,"I mean, the mental images thing really throws it."],[2019.14,"Because you can argue come out and, well, no, everyone's a sinner, and I'm like, okay, but what's the point of God giving a commandment that it's impossible for us to go five seconds without violating?"],[2034.63,"Like, how does that help us?"],[2037.35,"The first commandment we read."],[2039.13,"Even though we often violate it, myself included."],[2043.03,"At least by reading the commandment and thinking about it, we can start thinking about that, going, oh, you know what?"],[2049.19,"Yeah, I did."],[2051.11,"I need to rethink how."],[2052.92,"Like, I could use that helpfully, to repent and try to do better."],[2058.29,"How would that let you repent and try to do better?"],[2061.25,"It's not useful."],[2063.05,"It's impossible. It's impossible."],[2066.69,"And they're just about to make, they're gonna be making an arc here in the city."],[2069.81,"Yeah, yeah. And he's gonna tell them to make some things."],[2074.17,"You know? You can try to get around that saying by, well, if God tells you to do it, you're allowed to do it, like sacrificing your son."],[2080.1,"Like, you know."],[2081.3,"You can try that angle."],[2082.84,"But still, none of the folks who take that interpretation really live consistently with that interpretation."],[2087.4,"Because they wouldn't have any family photos, they wouldn't have any, they'd be grieved all the time about their mental images."],[2093.58,"Of anything. Um, you know, remembering their parents."],[2097.74,"Oh no, I've, you know."],[2099.9,"Um. So, what is I what is the point here with idolatry?"],[2107.43,"Okay, so first of all, an idol making an idol, as was understood in the ancient world, it would have been understood by the well, here this is you're making an image or a likeness of a spiritual being, of a god or a goddess."],[2122.97,"You're making an embodiment of it."],[2125.49,"And how did they embody them?"],[2129.49,"Well, in the likeness of some creature."],[2133.81,"So you're trying to embody the divine in the likeness of some created thing."],[2141.25,"St. Athanasius actually talks about this a lot at one point when he's critiquing the pagans of his own day in the fourth century."],[2148.03,"That you've taken the divine, you should know better."],[2150.67,"He's talking to Greek pagans, and he's like, You Greek pagans, even, you've read philosophy, you should know."],[2155.65,"You can't embody the divine in the likeness of a human or an animal."],[2161.99,"Like, that doesn't make any sense."],[2163.59,"But that was the idea."],[2166.41,"That was the idea."],[2167.29,"And often it's in sort of mixed human animal forms."],[2170.73,"Yeah, in Egypt."],[2172.01,"Like in Egypt."],[2172.98,"Um, and that was true."],[2175.22,"God's being depicted uh here's the new word for today."],[2180.98,"Theromorph. In the likeness of a beast."],[2186.74,"Yes. Theromorphic depictions of pagan gods were everywhere."],[2193.82,"It wasn't just the ancient Near East in Egypt."],[2196.7,"Even the Greek gods originally were in mixed human animal forms."],[2205.74,"Yeah, and then and then they transitioned to the likeness of certain human kings."],[2211.98,"They started depicting Zeus as a particular human king."],[2215.92,"They started depicting."],[2217.36,"But for example, you get little bits of this slipping out, like Homer refers to cow-eyed Hera."],[2222.72,"Yeah. Hera, you know, Zeus's wife."],[2227.01,"Um, I don't think, I mean, this may be a cultural difference, but I don't think there's a culture on Earth where. if you told a woman she had eyes like a cow, that would be considered a compliment"],[2240.49,"Um if you think that you haven't hung around with cows and watched them like stare at you while you're in the pasture."],[2247.45,"Um they do, but would you want to be told your eyes were like a cow's?"],[2255.45,"It depends on who say it."],[2256.81,"It'd have to be in the context of a lot of poetry."],[2259.05,"Yeah. So, but the point being this is a hold, this isn't just an analogy he's making, because he thinks cows are sweet, nice animals."],[2269.61,"It's right, a holdover from those earlier depictions of her as like a heifer and Zeus is a bull."],[2277.3,"That's a visual."],[2278.5,"the But so that is how gods are taken."],[2280.3,"So that it's a it's a holdover."],[2285.0,"Well, now why did they do this?"],[2287.48,"Why did they make these idols?"],[2289.97,"It wasn't because, oh, this is what I think this spirit actually looks like, and I want to be accurate."],[2297.27,"They're creating this thing because the image they created defined, and embodied that spirit so that they could interact with it."],[2310.05,"And by interact with it, I don't mean have a friendship, because people weren't friends with pagan gods."],[2316.05,"You weren't pals."],[2317.41,"It was to try to get them to do things."],[2319.49,"So an idol was sort of a spiritual lever."],[2324.82,"Depending on the pagan god."],[2326.6,"Some of them you just kind of wanted to placate."],[2329.96,"So you'd come and you'd feed them and clothe them and give them gifts to try and keep them from smiting you."],[2336.84,"To ward them off."],[2338.85,"And then other ones you would be trying to bribe for favors by doing some of the same things."],[2345.99,"To get them to building them a beautiful temple to put their statue in and everything right um and so they believe that that was their god now why is this that's why this is a separate commandment from the one we just read about having no other gods because we're gonna see it's not too many chapters before we get to the golden calf the Israelites aren't going to just build idols of those other gods the way the pagans did they're gonna try to make idols of Yahweh like the golden calf and Jeroboam later on with the golden calves at uh Bethel and Dan and why would they do that?"],[2387.17,"Why would they make a bull and say this is this is Yahweh to try to use it as a lever to try to get him to do what they wanted."],[2401.63,"Thinking that they could."],[2403.87,"Well, it looks like the golden calf looks the is was probably an apus bull."],[2408.59,"So there was something they got from Egypt that they just came out of from Egyptian culture."],[2414.51,"So is that like the idol situation akin to naming, like what Adam did?"],[2421.23,"Because if you can identify a thing, then you can kind of like it."],[2429.47,"To a point, you're like in the definitional sense."],[2432.29,"You're kind of taking an authority over it."],[2434.37,"You're putting trying to put yourself over it."],[2437.01,"But the whole creation of Adam's story is an inversion of how they made idols."],[2445.41,"So they would make an idol, they place it in the temple, and then the last step was they did a ritual called the opening of the mouth or sometimes the opening of the nose, where the spirit would enter into the idol, and then they could use the idol to control and manipulate."],[2462.26,"So when you look at the creation of Adam in Genesis 2, God makes the garden like a temple."],[2472.37,"God makes man in his image, and then he breathes the spirit into Adam."],[2479.33,"But so man as the image of God is the inverse of idolatry because it's not a way to get God to do something."],[2489.25,"It's right, it's that God now is going to do something through man."],[2496.95,"But then Adam names that animals, and that's kind of what I was."],[2501.59,"Yeah, that's an authority kingship thing."],[2504.63,"We talked about that back at the time."],[2506.23,"We have these Hittite murals of kings surrounded by wild animals holding a staff and naming them."],[2515.19,"So isn't so is like the idol making sort of like you say it's like a spiritual lever."],[2520.53,"So it's the person trying to take control of the, yeah, by defining it."],[2527.37,"Whereas God gave his name."],[2528.73,"That limits it."],[2529.61,"Yeah, God gave his name in the English."],[2531.59,"God is ineffable."],[2532.89,"He can't be defined, he can't be limited, comprehended."],[2536.07,"But by trying to one form, that's a limit."],[2541.95,"That's a definition in a certain way."],[2545.39,"And then you can get a handle on it to try to manipulate."],[2551.31,"Yeah. Why you needed so many gods."],[2553.55,"Yeah. And so a lot of times."],[2561.09,"Again, most of us are not going to go and uh, as we already said, this doesn't apply to things like iconography."],[2568.47,"Or family pictures."],[2570.09,"If I have a picture of my wife on my desk and you see me pick it up and kiss it, you're not going to be like, oh no, Father Stephen is having an affair with a two-ply coated piece of Bristol paper that he loves as much as his wife."],[2584.65,"You're gonna say, well, no, he was feeling love toward his wife, and he expressed it by kissing a picture of her."],[2590.83,"That's what you're gonna say, if you're a normal person."],[2593.97,"Like. So it's not talking about that."],[2599.95,"But what it does address, again, we don't do this gross thing."],[2603.63,"None of us are sitting there building a statue of Jesus and you know, giving it food."],[2609.55,"And there are places where they do that."],[2611.79,"Yeah. In Central and South America, but we won't get into that."],[2615.25,"Um that's offering it food."],[2618.54,"Dressing it up, trying to get it to do favors for us based on. like we're not doing it again in that gross sense"],[2626.68,"But a lot of us try to get a handle or a leper on God."],[2632.78,"In various ways."],[2634.7,"Well, if I do this, then"],[2637.08,"And there are even people who preach about God that way."],[2641.26,"Well, if you give, then God will give it back to you."],[2646.23,"As if God sort of operates by certain rules, and you can figure out what those rules are and use those rules to manipulate him."],[2655.24,"And we want to figure out."],[2656.52,"I once I once had somebody who was praying for something say, Well, do you have any like more powerful? prayers you can use"],[2666.72,"Because I really want this to happen."],[2668.24,"And I'm like, that's called magic, not prayers."],[2672.33,"Like, that's, you know, that there's some kind of oom for trick, or you know, uh that you can use that now, God will do what I want him to."],[2683.13,"Or, you know, if I can just get a thousand likes on this photo, then God will heal the kid."],[2689.39,"Like, oh no, 999, the kid's doomed."],[2693.77,"Um all of that is a form of idolatry."],[2699.54,"Anytime we're trying to get God to do what we want, and our prayer can very easily fall into that."],[2705.86,"You've heard me say before this Bible study"],[2708.68,"Sometimes our prayers sort of sound like reading God, our list of demands."],[2714.06,"I want this, I want this, I want this, take care of this, heal this person."],[2718.6,"Heal me, do this for me, do that for me."],[2721.42,"And that's about it."],[2723.11,"Amen. And then we get mad and disheartened that God doesn't answer our prayers, meaning he doesn't do what we wanted him to do."],[2733.02,"So idolatry is very easy for us to fall into, wanting to sort of take control of, get a handle on and take control of God, rather than letting God get a handle on and take control of us."],[2745.86,"Which might be a whole lot better."],[2746.62,"a whole lot And work out better for us in the long run."],[2752.12,"Than uh than the other way around."],[2759.32,"Okay, then it continues, you shall not bow down to them or serve them."],[2763.48,"For I the Lord your God am a jealous God, recompensing the sins of the fathers on the children to the third and fourth generation of those who hate me, but showing mercy to thousands to those who love me and keep my commandments."],[2784.09,"So, just not bow down to them and serve them."],[2786.33,"This is a good talk about worship."],[2788.26,"Don't worship idols."],[2789.38,"s. You can decide if those are two separate commandment or not"],[2789.9,"First of all, don't make them, don't worship them."],[2795.26,"Right? Um, but you don't do those things."],[2798.64,"Then he says this, because I'm a jealous God."],[2803.17,"Um, so this is this is obviously metaphorical."],[2809.17,"It's not that God's up there, like, oh, they're looking at other gods, I can tell."],[2814.87,"Like, but it is an analogy."],[2816.97,"It is an analogy because there's going to be this analogy throughout the Hebrew scriptures, throughout the Old Testament."],[2822.33,"So get used to it now, because it's going to get especially thick when we get into the prophets of Israel, God's people, as his bride, as his spouse."],[2832.42,"As his wife."],[2836.06,"And so, and he is going to compare them going after other gods."],[2842.72,"Whether it be, well, it's pretty much always idolatry, but also worshiping other gods."],[2848.48,"He's going to compare that to adultery."],[2851.84,"Sometimes in very graphic language."],[2854.64,"It's going to get awkward when we get into Ezekiel at a couple of points."],[2860.48,"The last time I went through those parts of Ezekiel, my sister was coming to my Bible study, and she just skipped a couple."],[2866.4,"She didn't want to hear me reading that stuff."],[2868.32,"It's her brother"],[2869.9,"But he gets very graphic about it."],[2872.2,"And this is part of that analogy."],[2874.52,"part of that analogy This is."],[2876.04,"Okay But now we we have to, we we now, in our modern culture, go figure, have very unhealthy views of relationships between men and women."],[2892.9,"So to us as modern people, that may sound like, oh, that guy's jealous and controlling."],[2899.87,"Like these are bad qualities."],[2902.85,"These are bad qualities."],[2904.69,"Okay. But we have to go a little bit outside the modern context."],[2911.87,"Outside the modern context."],[2913.79,"Think back then."],[2916.32,"What kind of husband would have his wife running around with other men and not care?"],[2932.29,"That would not be a good husband."],[2936.34,"That would not be a good husband."],[2939.7,"Why? Would that be good for the wife?"],[2944.5,"Think about it in the ancient world, where we're in an honor shame culture and stuff."],[2952.28,"That would be good for the wife."],[2953.64,"That would mean, frankly, that he didn't love her."],[2957.0,"That he didn't care about her."],[2959.64,"Yeah, go do whatever you want, I don't care."],[2963.38,"So that's the idea here."],[2965.48,"The idea is no, God cares."],[2968.7,"God loves them."],[2971.02,"Because he loves them, he's not going to let them go and engage in this kind of destructive behavior."],[2979.95,"He's not going to let those other gods have them."],[2982.51,"He's going to be protecting them from those demons or spread issues."],[2986.35,"It's like he protected them from the gods of Egypt and rescued them from them."],[2990.84,"But they need to not go running off after them either."],[2994.65,"That's what he's saying here."],[2997.13,"The other part that people get hung up on is how he's punishing the sins of the fathers to the third and fourth generation of those who hate him."],[3005.19,"But then what comes immediately after that?"],[3007.59,"Mercy to tell."],[3008.55,"Which only up to thousands."],[3010.01,"So the contrast is between."],[3013.59,"The contrast is between, yes, there's this justice part."],[3019.6,"But then there's this mercy part that's much bigger."],[3024.74,"That's much bigger."],[3028.98,"Okay. So in the context, if we take this back to this analogy of the wife, what does that mean?"],[3037.14,"That means, yes, he is going to be metaphorically angry, upset."],[3043.87,"If they go and do this."],[3046.67,"But there's also going to be this mercy."],[3051.47,"This much greater mercy."],[3053.81,"If they come back."],[3057.57,"And they love God and they struggle to keep his commandments."],[3063.5,"But that also gives us a picture here of repentance by how he describes it."],[3067.9,"Repentance is not just like, oh yeah, I'm sorry."],[3071.26,"So imagine our analogy again."],[3073.34,"We've got a husband and a wife."],[3075.02,"The wife keeps cheating, but every time she cheats, she comes back to the husband and says, Oh, I'm sorry."],[3083.02,"That's not repentance."],[3086.62,"Just as it would be clear if the husband didn't care about it that he didn't love her."],[3090.38,"It'd be pretty clear if she did that that she didn't love her husband."],[3095.87,"So, what is repentance here?"],[3098.67,"Loving him and keeping his commandments, which are the same thing."],[3103.39,"Jesus says this too."],[3104.83,"If you love me, you will keep my commandments."],[3128.51,"In terms of the scriptures."],[3130.47,"Uh in that women have gotten used to when the Bible says he, just knowing that that means he or she."],[3138.69,"That means people, humans, Christians."],[3140.95,"Whereas men, when it talks about like the church being the bride of Christ, we get all weird about it."],[3146.33,"Like, I don't know a lot of guys who want to say, like, yes, I am the bride of Christ."],[3150.17,"You know, they're like."],[3151.74,"We get hung up on that kind of stuff."],[3153.52,"But that's where we, as male Christians, fit into this metaphor."],[3158.08,"We are the right God's bride."],[3162.5,"So we have to be able to see ourselves in the feminine language of the Bible the same way women have learned to see themselves in the masculine language."],[3171.2,"When it's when it's in this kind of context."],[3174.01,"Um, but I have to say if I sort of but in the same way."],[3180.69,"Any wife can look at any husband and say, if you love me, you will be faithful."],[3186.13,"And if you aren't, that will be a sign to me that you don't really love me."],[3192.55,"Doesn't mean you never being faithful doesn't mean you never mess up or make a mistake."],[3197.85,"Or do anything."],[3198.81,"But being faithful means you always come back."],[3201.69,"And you always try and make it right."],[3205.69,"You always try to restore right what's been broken."],[3209.69,"That's what being faithful means."],[3212.57,"He's giving this covenant"],[3214.53,"And that's what God is going to want."],[3217.49,"He wants Israel to be faithful to it."],[3220.61,"Ultimately. Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God."],[3230.45,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3235.81,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.36,"lv":2,"h":"Recap and Exodus 20:1-3 - the first words","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Exodus 20</b> - famous chapter, \"especially around these parts, if you follow the local news\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Recap: Israel at <b>Sinai</b>, the Mountain of God; God descended; Moses up</li><li class=\"d1\">now God talking to Moses, issuing his <b>covenant</b> to the newly forming nation</li><li class=\"d0\">This is the <b>Ten Commandments</b> - well-known section</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:1-3</b>: \"The Lord spoke all these words… <b>I am the Lord your God</b> who brought you out of Egypt, house of bondage. <b>No other gods before me</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Only <b>nine</b> of them\" - nine commandments actually given in the text</li><li class=\"d1\">always called Ten → different groups <b>divide differently</b> to make ten</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jewish</b>, <b>Roman Catholic</b>, <b>Lutheran</b>, other <b>Protestant</b> ways</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Orthodox</b> division actually closest to the <b>Lutheran</b></li></ul>"},{"t":220.5,"lv":3,"h":"Ten words, not commandments","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Historically the <b>ten words</b> - Greek <i>deka logoi</i> → <b>decalogue</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"words\" gives flexibility: not all have to be commandments</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jewish numbering</b>: \"I am Yahweh your God who brought you out…\" = <b>#1</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's not a commandment\" - \"well, it's a <b>word</b>\"; \"the Lord spoke all these <b>words</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">All other numberings assume <b>commandments</b>: \"you shall / you shall not\"; King James \"<b>thou shalt not</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: why \"commandments\" rather than words? \"Lost in prehistory\"</li><li class=\"d1\">in pretty much every form of Christianity the Ten Commandments <b>loom large</b></li><li class=\"d1\">every <b>Protestant confession</b> from the Reformation has a section; Orthodox prayer book <b>guide for confession</b> starts with them; Roman Catholic too</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>basic building block</b>, basic moral principles</li></ul>"},{"t":383.3,"lv":2,"h":"Why Christianity elevated them","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not historically that role in <b>Judaism</b> - focused on <b>all</b> the commandments, don't differentiate</li><li class=\"d0\">Question we'll keep running into: what applies to <b>everyone incl. Gentiles</b> vs only the <b>Jewish people</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">rabbinic Judaism not concerned about Gentiles - all applies to them</li><li class=\"d1\">these: against <b>idolatry, pagan worship, murder, adultery, stealing</b> - wrong not because someone is Jewish</li><li class=\"d0\">His theory (\"no paper trail\"): early church → this grouping took a <b>special place</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what follows: ch. 21 \"<b>Buying a Slave</b>\", owners of animals - doesn't apply to <b>Gentile Christians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"this is where God starts\" → unit that matters to us; \"words\" emphasis faded</li><li class=\"d1\">commandments <b>we</b> need to be concerned about more than those that follow</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Believable. Plausible. Could be true.\"</li></ul>"},{"t":534.37,"lv":2,"h":"How to get ten: splitting one of the nine","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">To reach ten, <b>split one</b> of the nine, two ways:</li><li class=\"d1\">split <b>idols</b> (v. 4-5): don't make / don't bow down or serve - \"weird split\"</li><li class=\"d1\">split <b>coveting</b> (v. 17): neighbor's wife vs neighbor's stuff - \"doesn't make a heck of a lot of sense\"</li><li class=\"d1\">all about coveting; already a commandment against <b>adultery</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Roman Catholic split has a problem: <b>Deuteronomy 5</b> repeats them, wording not identical</li><li class=\"d1\">Deut 5 lists <b>house</b> first → Deut 5 and Exod 20 would have a <b>different ninth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">His answer: \"I'm <b>cool with it just being nine</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"is the numbering that important?\" vs understanding what God tells us to do and not do</li><li class=\"d0\">Q (classroom - need an even number): <b>idol making / idol worship</b> - do you divide? \"Usually. Not always\"</li></ul>"},{"t":732.97,"lv":3,"h":"Ancient manuscripts, ten words, Hebrew <i>lo</i>","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hard to tell: <b>ancient Greek manuscripts</b> look nothing like a Greek text you buy now</li><li class=\"d1\">modern: spaces, capital and lowercase, accents - manuscripts <b>have none</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no spaces, no sentence-ending punctuation → how sure where the fathers divide?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"context clues… maybe he's dividing these two. Possibly\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Don't get hung up on it - \"what's being communicated, not the number\"</li><li class=\"d1\">still says <b>Ten Commandments</b> (\"nine commandments\" → weird looks); or \"<b>ten words</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Hebrew for \"thou shalt not kill\"? - particle <b><i>lo</i></b> negates a sentence</li><li class=\"d1\">no real verb tenses; \"<b>no</b> you kill, <b>no</b> you steal\" if woodenly literal</li><li class=\"d1\">positives (\"honor your father and mother\") have <b>no <i>lo</i></b> - no \"thou shalt not\", yet a commandment</li></ul>"},{"t":930.52,"lv":2,"h":"Two tablets = two copies, not half and half","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Covenants, <b>Hittite treaties</b>, <b>two tablets</b> - mentioned already; people nodding</li><li class=\"d0\">Popular: <b>first four</b> on one tablet (God), <b>other six</b> on the other (fellow humans)</li><li class=\"d1\">Mel Brooks, <i>History of the World</i>: <b>15 Commandments</b>, drops one, \"Ten\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"all this stuff drawn out\" about God vs neighbor</li><li class=\"d0\">Reality: <b>two copies of the same thing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">pagan treaty: one copy to the <b>king</b> who had to read and follow it</li><li class=\"d1\">other at the <b>feet of the idol of that nation's god</b> in the temple → god as <b>witness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Yahweh = <b>king and God of Israel</b> → both end up in the <b>Ark of the Covenant</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Ark mentioned earlier in the text, before told how to make it</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Both copies are put at <b>God's feet</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1070.56,"lv":3,"h":"The tablets were small clay","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Father Stephen Ruin Sunday School\": tablets probably <b>much smaller</b> than thought</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Charlton Heston</b>'s big stone ones - \"styrofoam painted gray\"; he couldn't hold real stone that size</li><li class=\"d0\">Clay tablets made <b>in the palm of your hand</b> - rounded on the back</li><li class=\"d1\">smooth clay in your hand; <b>stylus</b> with the other hand while wet (wedge marks)</li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>fired in a kiln</b> → last \"pretty much forever\", 5,000 years; throw one down and it dents the floor</li><li class=\"d0\">Written by God's <b>finger</b> - \"how big is God's hand?\" → \"how big is <b>Jesus' hand</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that's who Moses is talking to</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Human palm-sized</b>, relatively small; two of them</li><li class=\"d1\">fits <b>Ark of the Covenant measurements</b> - Charlton Heston size wouldn't fit</li></ul>"},{"t":1226.52,"lv":2,"h":"The treaty format and 20:2","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Format: treaty issued by <b>suzerain</b> (great king) to a <b>vassal king</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>who I am</b>; <b>what I've done</b> (pagan ones: peace, prosperity, defeated enemies)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>responsibilities</b> - tribute of slaves, oxen, harvest, so much wheat</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>blessings</b> if kept, <b>curses</b> if not</li><li class=\"d0\">Already followed here; <b>Deuteronomy</b> follows it as a whole</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I am <b>Yahweh your God</b>\" = names himself; \"who brought you out of Egypt\" = what I did for you</li><li class=\"d1\">then \"here's what's expected\" - the commandments</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: each person treated as a king / vassal, \"not like a serf\" → <b>kingdom of priests</b>, royal priesthood elevates them</li><li class=\"d1\">normally king to king, not to all the people</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not a covenant between God and Moses</b> - between God and <b>his people, mediated through Moses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">NT: Moses = <b>mediator of the covenant</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1407.41,"lv":2,"h":"20:3 - No other gods before my face","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Arguably the <b>first commandment</b>\": \"no other gods before me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">wooden literal: \"no other gods <b>in my face</b>\" - face = \"presence\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The <b>face of God</b> is common in the OT - \"every time it talks about God's presence\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jonah</b> flees from the <b>face of God</b>; priests serve before the <b>face of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>the God nobody has seen</b> - constant tension in the Hebrew idiom</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses speaks <b>face to face</b>; 9 verses later: \"<b>no one can see my face and live</b>\" - \"hmm?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not saying other gods don't exist - not \"imaginary\"; cf. <b>judging the gods of Egypt</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you don't bring any of them <b>into my presence</b>\" - can't worship God <b>alongside</b> other things</li><li class=\"d0\">Application: Christ - \"<b>No man can serve two masters</b>\" (not polygamy) - <b>God and mammon</b>; \"serve\" = worship</li></ul>"},{"t":1589.1,"lv":3,"h":"Divided loyalties and nuancing our faith","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not \"I don't worship Zeus or Hindu gods\" - different temptation</li><li class=\"d1\">not good worshipers of the true God vs bad others → about <b>divided loyalties</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ pushes it: \"loves father or mother <b>more than me</b> is not worthy\"; <b>Luke</b>: \"unless you <b>hate</b> your father and mother\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hard for modern people - we <b>nuance our faith</b> by other factors</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm a Christian, but my son/daughter/brother does XYZ\" - love, but wrong? or \"not really wrong\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">loving them → put <b>above</b> our loyalty to what God said</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a guy's got to make a living… capitalist society… investments doing well… <b>God's blessing me</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Always the <b>faith</b> that gets bent and warped, rather than what we do</li><li class=\"d1\">OT unsubtle: altar to Yahweh, <b>altar to Baal</b> right next to it; we do it in \"subtle, clever, nuanced ways\" so we can pretend</li></ul>"},{"t":1817.45,"lv":2,"h":"20:4 - No idol or likeness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Didn't tackle at end of last study</li><li class=\"d0\">\"You shall not make for yourself an <b>idol</b> or a <b>likeness</b> of anything in heaven above, earth beneath, waters under the earth\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Some \"think they're taking this very literally - and they're not\"</li><li class=\"d1\">say it's about <b>images</b>: <b>icons</b> in Orthodox church; a <b>movie with Jesus</b> (even animated)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>flannel graphs</b> in Sunday school</li><li class=\"d0\">Text is about <b>idols</b> - how idolatry works to come</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek/Hebrew: \"or\" - really <b>an idol in the likeness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Q: not even a swan statue in the garden? - he doesn't think anyone really holds that</li></ul>"},{"t":1920.35,"lv":3,"h":"Problem with the literal reading","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">If you go that road you really have to: <b>no family photos, portraits, toys, landscapes</b> - trees are creatures</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mental images</b> - gospel reading, you picture Jesus → \"just made an idol\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Everyone's a sinner\" - then why give a commandment <b>impossible to keep five seconds</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">first commandment: even though we violate it, reading it lets us <b>repent</b> and do better</li><li class=\"d1\">an impossible one can't help us repent</li><li class=\"d0\">They're about to make the <b>Ark</b>; he'll tell them to make things</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if God tells you to do it, you can\" (sacrificing your son) - try that angle</li><li class=\"d0\">None who hold it live consistently - no family photos, grieved over mental images of <b>parents</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2099.9,"lv":2,"h":"What idolatry actually is","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ancient idol = image of a <b>spiritual being</b>, god or goddess → an <b>embodiment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">embodying the divine in the <b>likeness of some created thing</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Athanasius</b> critiques 4th-c. pagans: \"you Greek pagans, you've read philosophy, you should know\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't embody the divine in the likeness of a human or animal</li><li class=\"d0\">New word: <b><i>theriomorph</i></b> = in the likeness of a beast</li><li class=\"d1\">theriomorphic depictions everywhere: <b>Egypt</b>, ancient Near East, even <b>Greek gods</b> originally mixed human-animal</li><li class=\"d1\">later depicted as human kings - <b>Zeus</b> as a particular king</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Homer</b>: \"<b>cow-eyed Hera</b>\" - holdover from Hera as heifer, Zeus as bull</li><li class=\"d1\">no culture where \"eyes like a cow\" is a compliment - \"needs a lot of poetry\"; cows stare at you in the pasture</li></ul>"},{"t":2285.0,"lv":2,"h":"Idols as spiritual levers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why make idols? Not \"this is what the spirit looks like\" - accuracy</li><li class=\"d1\">the image <b>defined and embodied</b> the spirit so they could <b>interact</b> with it</li><li class=\"d1\">not friendship - \"you weren't <b>pals</b>\"; to get them to do things</li><li class=\"d0\">An idol = <b>a spiritual lever</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some gods you <b>placate</b>: feed, clothe, gifts \"to keep them from smiting you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">others you <b>bribe for favors</b> - beautiful temple for the statue</li><li class=\"d0\">Why a <b>separate</b> commandment from no other gods:</li><li class=\"d1\">Israel won't just build idols of other gods - they'll make idols of <b>Yahweh</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>golden calf</b> (not many chapters away); <b>Jeroboam's calves at Bethel and Dan</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"a bull… this is Yahweh\" as a lever to get him to do what they wanted; probably an <b>Apis bull</b> from Egypt</li></ul>"},{"t":2414.51,"lv":3,"h":"Q: naming, the opening of the mouth, ineffability","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: idol akin to <b>Adam naming</b> - identify a thing, take authority over it?</li><li class=\"d0\">Creation of Adam = <b>inversion</b> of idol making</li><li class=\"d1\">idol made, placed in temple, last step <b>opening of the mouth</b> (or nose) ritual → spirit enters → <b>control and manipulate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis 2: garden like a temple; man in God's image; <b>God breathes the spirit</b> into Adam</li><li class=\"d1\">man as image = inverse of idolatry: not a way to get God to do something, but <b>God does something through man</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Adam naming = <b>kingship</b>; <b>Hittite murals</b> of kings with staff among wild animals naming them</li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: idol making = control by <b>defining</b>; God gave his name…</li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>ineffable</b> - can't be defined, limited, comprehended</li><li class=\"d1\">one form = a limit → \"a handle\" to manipulate; \"why you needed so many gods\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2561.09,"lv":2,"h":"Modern idolatry: handles on God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't apply to <b>iconography</b> or <b>family pictures</b></li><li class=\"d1\">kiss a picture of your wife - \"affair with a two-ply coated piece of Bristol paper\"? No - <b>love expressed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">None of us build a statue of Jesus and feed it (some places do - Central/South America)</li><li class=\"d0\">But many try to get <b>a handle or lever on God</b>:</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if I do this, then…\"; preachers: \"<b>if you give, God will give it back</b>\" - figure out the rules and manipulate him</li><li class=\"d1\">\"do you have any <b>more powerful prayers</b>?\" → \"that's called <b>magic</b>, not prayer\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a thousand likes on this photo and God will heal the kid… 999, the kid's doomed\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Prayer can sound like a <b>list of demands</b> → get mad and disheartened that God didn't do what we wanted</li><li class=\"d0\">Idolatry = trying to take control of God <b>rather than letting God take control of us</b> - \"a whole lot better in the long run\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2759.32,"lv":2,"h":"20:5 - Jealous God and the marriage analogy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Don't <b>bow down</b> or <b>serve</b>\" = worship; maybe two commandments</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I the Lord your God am a <b>jealous God</b>\" - obviously <b>metaphorical</b>: \"not that God's up there, oh they're looking at other gods\"</li><li class=\"d0\">An analogy throughout the OT, thick in the <b>prophets</b>: God's people as his <b>bride / spouse / wife</b></li><li class=\"d1\">other gods (idolatry, worshiping other gods) = <b>adultery</b>, sometimes graphic - awkward in <b>Ezekiel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">his sister coming to Bible study skipped parts</li><li class=\"d0\">Modern unhealthy views - \"jealous and controlling\" = bad qualities</li><li class=\"d1\">go outside the modern context: honor-shame ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">husband who didn't care if his wife ran around with other men = <b>didn't love her</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God <b>cares</b>, loves them → won't let them go to destructive behavior</li><li class=\"d1\">protects them from <b>demons</b>, as he rescued them from the gods of Egypt; they must not run after them</li></ul>"},{"t":2997.13,"lv":3,"h":"Sins of the fathers - and mercy to thousands","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">People hung up: \"<b>sins of the fathers to the third and fourth generation</b> of those who hate me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Immediately after: <b>mercy to thousands</b> - the contrast</li><li class=\"d1\">justice part, but the mercy part is <b>much bigger</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Wife analogy: <b>metaphorically angry</b> if they do this; <b>much greater mercy</b> if they come back, love God, struggle to keep his commandments</li><li class=\"d0\">Picture of <b>repentance</b> - not just \"oh yeah, I'm sorry\"</li><li class=\"d1\">wife keeps cheating, each time says sorry → not repentance; shows she doesn't love her husband</li><li class=\"d1\">as clear as it was if the husband didn't care</li><li class=\"d0\">Repentance = <b>loving him and keeping his commandments, the same thing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus: \"<b>if you love me, you will keep my commandments</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":3128.51,"lv":3,"h":"Men as the bride; faithfulness means coming back","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Women are used to \"he\" in Scripture meaning people, Christians</li><li class=\"d1\">men get weird about <b>church as the bride of Christ</b> - \"I don't know many guys who want to say I am the bride of Christ\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Male Christians fit into the metaphor as God's bride</b></li><li class=\"d1\">must see ourselves in <b>feminine language</b> as women learned to in masculine</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Any wife can look at any husband and say: <b>if you love me, you will be faithful</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not faithful = sign you don't love me</li><li class=\"d1\">faithful ≠ never mess up or make a mistake - <b>you always come back</b></li><li class=\"d1\">always try to <b>make it right</b>, <b>restore what's been broken</b></li><li class=\"d0\">That's what God wants - he's giving this covenant; wants <b>Israel faithful</b> to it, ultimately</li></ul>"}]});
